# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT COMMITTED
#
# The rule: anything that is EVIDENCE stays in git; anything that is bulk,
# machine-local or exactly re-derivable from committed evidence does not.
# results/ledgers/, results/snapshots/ and results/events/ ARE evidence and are
# committed — every headline count must be re-derivable offline from plain text.

# Isolated experiment databases — disposable by design; `clean_dbs` removes them.
dbs/

# Downloaded PDFs: large, and fully described by the committed data/REGISTRY.json
# (versioned URL + sha256 + ETag), so they are re-fetchable rather than stored.
data/pdfs/

# Docling output: regenerated by `rule convert_reference`, large, and explicitly
# NOT byte-stable across docling versions — committing it would imply otherwise.
data/markdown/

# Deliberately-broken fixtures, materialised by claim_f_fail at run time.
data/bad_sources/

# Worker logs: dominated by RapidOCR/Docling progress noise, megabytes per run.
# The facts extracted from them live in results/claim_c_*.json.
logs/

# Snakemake internals and the optional (demoted) HTML reporter.
.snakemake/
report_snakemake.html
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Verbatim per-run archives — history is kept by `rule archive` locally, not in git.
runs/
